<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Colony of Vancouver Island</h1><p class="st-meta">1849 CE – 1866 CE · capital Victoria</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A British colony granted to the Hudson's Bay Company in 1849 to secure the Pacific coast north of the 49th parallel, with its capital at Victoria.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oregon-treaty">Oregon Treaty</a> of 1846 left <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> only the lands north of the 49th parallel, the Crown made Vancouver Island a colony in 1849 and leased it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company">Hudson's Bay Company</a> on condition that it bring settlers. Fort <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-victoria">Victoria</a>, founded by the Company in 1843, became the capital. The first governor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-blanshard">Richard Blanshard</a>, resigned within two years.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-douglas-governor">James Douglas</a>, the Company's chief factor, governed from 1851 to 1864. Between 1850 and 1854 he concluded fourteen land purchase agreements, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douglas-treaties">Douglas Treaties</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lekwungen">Lekwungen</a> and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-nations-in-canada">First Nations</a>. A small elected assembly, the first in western <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-canada">Canada</a>, met from 1856. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">Coal</a> mines at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanaimo">Nanaimo</a> and farming supplied the colony, but settlers were few until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fraser-river">Fraser River</a> gold rush of 1858 turned <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-victoria">Victoria</a> into a boom town.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Company's lease ended in 1859. Falling revenues after the gold rushes, and the costs of two separate governments, led <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> to merge the island with the mainland as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-british-columbia-1858-1866">Colony of British Columbia</a> in 1866.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/colony-of-vancouver-island.jpg" alt="Colony of Vancouver Island" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: Original: Acts of Union 1800 Vector: Yaddah · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_United_Kingdom_(3-5).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1849 CE – 1866 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Victoria</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Richard Blanshard, James Douglas, Arthur Kennedy</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Anglicanism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Vancouver Island</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1858&amp;focus=colony-of-vancouver-island" aria-label="Colony of Vancouver Island on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1817 485 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Colony of Vancouver Island lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2137 741L4997 651M2137 741L2166 718"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4997 651h0M2166 718h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2137 741h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2137 741h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Victoria. <a href="/atlas?year=1858&amp;focus=colony-of-vancouver-island">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
